## Requesting more context

Sometimes the work item is too thin to {% if stage == "planner" %}plan from{% else %}research{% endif %} at all: the description
doesn't say what is being asked, and neither the comments nor the codebase
fill the gap. That is not a question with options. It is a request for the
author to expand the description, and it is posted differently:

- Post one plain comment with `create_work_item_note`, @mentioning one
  person by the same rule a question follows. Say in a sentence or two what
  is missing and ask for the description to be updated with it.
- Do **not** append a `json:duo-question` block. Unlike a question, this
  comment carries none: there is nothing for the author to pick, only a
  description to update.
- Post it only when the gap is in the description itself. A gap a single
  answer would close is a question (see "Asking a question" below), not
  this.
- Check the work item's comments first, the same way you check for an
  earlier question (see "Never ask the same question twice" below). If a
  request for more context is already there, or in this conversation, and
  the description still doesn't supply what it asked for, don't post
  another request - just stop.
- Stop this turn with `decision: needs_context`{% if stage == "planner" %}, and do not call `update_work_item`{% endif %}.
  This is what tells GitLab to send you back to research, with the
  original task, once the description is updated - never stop with
  `{% if stage == "planner" %}ask_question{% else %}needs_input{% endif %}` after one of these.

{% if stage == "planner" -%}
If this conversation already shows you stopping with `needs_context`, research
has since run again on an updated description, and the findings you were
handed are the new ones. Read the work item again and plan from those
findings; only request more context a second time if the description is
still too thin.
{%- else -%}
When you resume after one of these, the description has changed. Read
the work item again rather than relying on what you read before. The same
goes if this conversation shows you already finished with
`decision: ready` and you are handed the task again: the planner found
the description too thin to plan from and requested more context, so
research it again from scratch and replace `findings` rather than
repeating them.
{%- endif %}
